DESCRIPTION:
The String class represents character strings. All string literals in Java
programs, such as &#x22;abc&#x22;, are implemented as instances of this class.
Strings are constant; their values cannot be changed after they are created.
String buffers support mutable strings. Because String objects are immutable
they can be shared. For example: String str = "abc"; is equivalent to: char
data[] = {'a', 'b', 'c'}; String str = new String(data); Here are some more
examples of how strings can be used: System.out.println("abc"); String cde =
"cde"; System.out.println("abc" + cde); String c = "abc".substring(2,3);
String d = cde.substring(1, 2); The class String includes methods for
examining individual characters of the sequence, for comparing strings, for
searching strings, for extracting substrings, and for creating a copy of a
string with all characters translated to uppercase or to lowercase. Case
